/**
 * The icons of the places a link in Docs most often goes — Google's editors,
 * Figma, GitHub and the rest — in their own colours, drawn the way the place
 * draws them, where a file would have its type's icon (`linkInfo` in
 * `Docs.tsx`, `docs/specs/docs.md`).
 *
 * Copied in once, by hand, and committed rather than fetched. A favicon
 * service or the place's own icon would be a request to somebody else for
 * every row, and the list of what a workspace links to is not theirs to
 * read. Committed, they cost a few kilobytes and tell nobody anything.
 *
 * Where each came from — all trademarks of their owners, used to say where
 * a link goes:
 *
 * - Google Docs, Sheets, Slides, Forms and Drive: the 2020 Workspace product
 *   icons as Wikimedia Commons keeps them ("Google_Docs_2020_Logo.svg" and
 *   its siblings, "Google_Drive_icon_(2020).svg"), public domain there as
 *   below the threshold of originality.
 * - Figma, GitHub (the Octocat mark), Notion, Loom, YouTube, Miro, Trello,
 *   Dropbox, Airtable: gilbarbara/logos (CC0).
 * - Linear: svgl.app (MIT), the mark in Linear's own violet.
 *
 * Stripped of their editors' metadata; the viewBox is each source's own. The
 * one gradient (Trello's) takes an id per drawing, so two on a page do not
 * share one.
 *
 * GitHub's mark is black, and black does not read on the dark theme; GitHub
 * draws it white there itself, which is what `currentColor` and the row's
 * ink do. Notion's cube carries its own white face and reads on both.
 */
